WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.509
Imagine an AI-controlled delivery robot in a corridor.

00:00:03.509 --> 00:00:05.523
This is a hypothetical example.

00:00:05.523 --> 00:00:10.980
A person steps into its route, and the robot adjusts its path while keeping the same

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.760
destination.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.787
That correction looks useful.

00:00:13.787 --> 00:00:17.220
But imagine the destination has been set to the wrong room.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.049
The robot could keep correcting its movements while still delivering the package to

00:00:22.049 --> 00:00:22.980
the wrong place.

00:00:23.317 --> 00:00:28.217
The safety question is whether competent pursuit leads to a useful outcome for

00:00:28.217 --> 00:00:28.657
people.

00:00:28.657 --> 00:00:33.871
To think clearly about that question, we first need a way to describe goal-directed

00:00:33.871 --> 00:00:34.437
machines.

00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:40.437
In January 1943, Arturo Rosenblueth, Norbert Wiener and Julian Bigelow asked how

00:00:40.437 --> 00:00:45.102
behavior could be classified across machines and living organisms.

00:00:45.102 --> 00:00:50.543
Their essay placed purpose and feedback at the center of that classification.

00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:55.849
They described behavior through its relation to a goal, and distinguished ongoing

00:00:55.849 --> 00:00:57.504
correction from prediction.

00:00:57.504 --> 00:01:02.163
How can a machine pursue a goal through a mechanism we can actually examine?

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.139
We will follow three ideas:

00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:11.320
what counts as purpose, how feedback corrects error, and what prediction adds.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:14.868
The authors start with observable behavior:

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:19.280
what changes around an object, and how those changes relate to inputs.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.510
Their behavioristic approach sets aside the object’s internal construction to study

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.519
those relations.

00:01:25.859 --> 00:01:30.867
They separate active behavior, where the object supplies the output energy, from

00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.121
passive behavior, where it does not.

00:01:33.121 --> 00:01:38.380
Within active behavior, they ask whether an action can be interpreted as approaching

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:38.819
a goal.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:43.232
Purpose here means a final relation to another object or event.

00:01:43.232 --> 00:01:47.306
It is a description of behavior directed toward that condition.

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:51.639
The definition does not tell us whether the condition is desirable.

00:01:51.975 --> 00:01:53.806
Consider the paper’s clock.

00:01:53.806 --> 00:01:59.094
Someone built it for a purpose, yet the authors classify its orderly motion as

00:01:59.094 --> 00:02:00.111
non-purposeful:

00:02:00.111 --> 00:02:03.975
the hands are not approaching a specific final condition.

00:02:04.325 --> 00:02:08.733
Their contrasting example is a torpedo with a target-seeking mechanism.

00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:13.762
They call such machines intrinsically purposeful, and use the term servomechanism

00:02:13.762 --> 00:02:16.245
for machines with this kind of behavior.

00:02:16.592 --> 00:02:21.826
The authors acknowledge that this broad definition of purpose is vague and difficult

00:02:21.826 --> 00:02:23.010
to apply precisely.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:28.182
They keep it as a useful starting point, then distinguish mechanisms we can examine

00:02:28.182 --> 00:02:28.992
more closely.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.668
We have examined purpose;

00:02:31.668 --> 00:02:34.650
next comes feedback and error correction.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.412
Negative feedback uses information about the gap between the current state and a

00:02:40.412 --> 00:02:40.719
goal.

00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:45.448
In the authors’ definition, signals from the goal restrict outputs that would

00:02:45.448 --> 00:02:46.860
otherwise go beyond it.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.638
A loop becomes visible:

00:02:48.638 --> 00:02:53.766
movement changes the relation to the goal, that relation supplies an error signal,

00:02:53.766 --> 00:02:56.331
and the signal changes the next movement.

00:02:56.331 --> 00:02:59.520
The correction depends on the remaining difference.

00:02:59.867 --> 00:03:04.813
The paper also allows purposeful behavior without feedback during the movement.

00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:07.818
Goal information can direct behavior beforehand.

00:03:07.818 --> 00:03:12.827
The distinction is whether fresh signals from the goal modify activity while the

00:03:12.827 --> 00:03:14.267
action is taking place.

00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:20.006
The authors argue that continuous feedback is especially useful when a goal moves.

00:03:20.006 --> 00:03:25.067
But they warn that correction can become clumsy if it is inadequately damped.

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:28.617
Damping means restraining oscillation in the response.

00:03:28.967 --> 00:03:33.014
They ask us to imagine a machine guided toward a moving light.

00:03:33.014 --> 00:03:38.433
It overshoots, receives a stronger signal in the opposite direction, and overshoots

00:03:38.433 --> 00:03:38.824
again.

00:03:38.824 --> 00:03:42.807
In their example, growing oscillations make it miss the goal.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.561
The machine remains goal-directed throughout this imagined failure.

00:03:47.561 --> 00:03:52.762
Having a goal and receiving correction signals do not, by themselves, make that

00:03:52.762 --> 00:03:54.672
particular motion successful.

00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:56.910
The way the feedback acts matters.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.091
We have traced feedback correction;

00:04:00.091 --> 00:04:01.830
now we turn to prediction.

00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:05.486
Feedback behavior can also be predictive.

00:04:05.486 --> 00:04:10.987
The authors contrast following a source with moving toward an anticipated future

00:04:10.987 --> 00:04:11.606
position.

00:04:11.606 --> 00:04:14.907
Their example is a cat pursuing a running mouse.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.096
In their account, the cat heads toward where the mouse is going.

00:04:19.096 --> 00:04:23.603
The diagram changes from a line to the current target into a route toward a

00:04:23.603 --> 00:04:25.045
projected meeting point.

00:04:25.045 --> 00:04:28.290
Prediction changes which position guides the movement.

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:31.954
Some tasks require anticipating more than one path.

00:04:31.954 --> 00:04:34.429
The cat predicts the mouse’s movement.

00:04:34.429 --> 00:04:39.898
Throwing a stone at a moving target also requires anticipating the stone’s movement,

00:04:39.898 --> 00:04:42.633
so both trajectories enter the prediction.

00:04:42.983 --> 00:04:46.568
Prediction requires information about time and space.

00:04:46.568 --> 00:04:49.003
The paper says sensing can limit it.

00:04:49.003 --> 00:04:54.483
A machine also needs internal organization capable of interpreting the signals it

00:04:54.483 --> 00:04:55.091
receives;

00:04:55.091 --> 00:04:59.623
sensitivity to light alone is insufficient for predictive tracking.

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:04.854
The authors argue that the same behavioral analysis can apply to machines and

00:05:04.854 --> 00:05:05.489
organisms.

00:05:05.489 --> 00:05:09.678
They also emphasize deep differences in their internal structures.

00:05:09.678 --> 00:05:14.311
A shared way of describing behavior is compatible with different physical

00:05:14.311 --> 00:05:15.327
implementations.

00:05:15.667 --> 00:05:19.950
The authors call purpose controlled by negative feedback teleology.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:25.256
Here, that word names correction guided by the difference between the present state

00:05:25.256 --> 00:05:25.959
and a goal.

00:05:25.959 --> 00:05:30.307
They leave broader questions of causality outside the essay’s scope.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.480
We have completed purpose, feedback and prediction;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.830
let us return to the delivery robot.

00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:40.251
Return to our hypothetical delivery robot.

00:05:40.251 --> 00:05:45.236
We can now distinguish its destination, the feedback that corrects its route, and

00:05:45.236 --> 00:05:48.067
prediction of where a moving obstacle will be.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.109
That vocabulary separates questions we might otherwise blur.

00:05:52.109 --> 00:05:53.840
Is movement being corrected?

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.189
Is it approaching the set destination?

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:59.156
Is that destination the one the person intended?

00:05:59.156 --> 00:06:03.360
The last question still matters even when the route looks competent.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:09.271
The January 1943 essay provides a conceptual classification with examples and

00:06:09.271 --> 00:06:10.066
hypotheses.

00:06:10.066 --> 00:06:15.347
It does not test modern AI systems or establish how to choose safe goals.

00:06:15.347 --> 00:06:18.820
That is the evidence boundary of this explainer.

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:20.696
The takeaway is simple:

00:06:20.696 --> 00:06:26.147
describing a machine as purposeful becomes more useful when we can trace its goal,

00:06:26.147 --> 00:06:28.674
its error signals and its corrections.

00:06:28.674 --> 00:06:33.727
For safety, we must also examine what outcome that goal leads to for people.
